WEBVTT
NOTE Cedwyn film, frame 004, en. Timings estimated from the script until the clip is rendered (re-split 27 Oct 2026: <=2x42 chars, <=20 cps, <=180 wpm).

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.211
<v Nia>Bore da. Hundreds of graves on
this one hill. Who were they?

2
00:00:04.331 --> 00:00:09.331
<v Nia>The dig is in Medieval Archaeology,
volume forty-nine: Holbrook and Thomas.

3
00:00:09.751 --> 00:00:14.663
<v a monk of Llandough>[in Welsh] Old stones lie under the
turf here; my spade still finds them.

4
00:00:14.783 --> 00:00:16.116
<v a monk of Llandough>Another grave today.

5
00:00:16.536 --> 00:00:20.803
<v Nia>The diggers came in nineteen
ninety-four, expecting a small job.

6
00:00:20.923 --> 00:00:25.923
<v Nia>Listen: 'At an early stage it became
clear that the site contained numerous

7
00:00:26.043 --> 00:00:30.843
<v Nia>burials that continued beyond the
bounds of the agreed excavation area.'

8
00:00:31.263 --> 00:00:32.796
<v a monk of Llandough>Beyond the bounds, aye.

9
00:00:32.916 --> 00:00:37.828
<v a monk of Llandough>Every winter takes someone, and every
one of them is laid on this hill,

10
00:00:37.948 --> 00:00:40.755
<v a monk of Llandough>row after row, looking down on the Ely.

11
00:00:41.175 --> 00:00:46.439
<v Nia>So they widened the trench to point
two two of a hectare, and counted one

12
00:00:46.559 --> 00:00:51.425
<v Nia>thousand and twenty-six burials before
work stopped in September nineteen

13
00:00:51.545 --> 00:00:52.845
<v Nia>ninety-four.

14
00:00:53.265 --> 00:00:54.565
<v Nia>Mind,

15
00:00:54.685 --> 00:01:00.019
<v Nia>the Historic Environment Record gives
eight hundred and fifty-eight inhumations.

16
00:01:00.139 --> 00:01:01.893
<v Nia>Two counts, both in print.

17
00:01:02.013 --> 00:01:05.873
<v Nia>We keep both on the page until
someone explains the gap.

18
00:01:06.293 --> 00:01:09.093
<v a monk of Llandough>Numbers. I know faces. The shepherd's boy.

19
00:01:09.213 --> 00:01:11.669
<v a monk of Llandough>The old woman from over the water.

20
00:01:11.789 --> 00:01:13.722
<v a monk of Llandough>The ground keeps taking them.

21
00:01:14.142 --> 00:01:18.342
<v Nia>Coflein, the Royal Commission's
record eight-oh-two-oh-nine-oh,

22
00:01:18.462 --> 00:01:22.062
<v Nia>files the cemetery; project
archive CA zero seventeen.

23
00:01:22.182 --> 00:01:26.249
<v Nia>The datasets are free online at
the Archaeology Data Service.

24
00:01:26.669 --> 00:01:29.125
<v a monk of Llandough>And the church? We pray in it.

25
00:01:29.245 --> 00:01:32.754
<v a monk of Llandough>Who built it first,
and when, I could not swear.

26
00:01:32.874 --> 00:01:34.979
<v a monk of Llandough>It was old when I came.

27
00:01:35.399 --> 00:01:39.732
<v Nia>The family's Master Timeline on
the Legal Wiki puts St Dochdwy's,

28
00:01:39.852 --> 00:01:44.519
<v Nia>beside the farm, at about six-fifty,
thought to be an early monastery.

29
00:01:44.639 --> 00:01:49.200
<v Nia>That's a summary, not a dig,
and its evidence folder is still empty.

30
00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.420
<v Nia>The same volume sets Llandough in context.

31
00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:56.940
<v Nia>The graves show a great early-medieval
burial ground on this hill.

32
00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:00.920
<v Nia>They don't show who ran it,
its name, or any deed.

33
00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:06.252
<v a monk of Llandough>Then write us down as we were:
graves above the Ely, a little church,

34
00:02:06.372 --> 00:02:07.672
<v a monk of Llandough>psalms at dusk.
